const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Which day trips from Nairobi are actually realistic in one day?',
    a: 'Lake Naivasha, Hell&rsquo;s Gate National Park, Mount Longonot, the Ngong Hills, Olorgesailie and the Limuru tea farms all work comfortably as single days from Nairobi. Lake Nakuru National Park works as a long day. Amboseli National Park is possible in one very long day and is better as an overnight, because the drive is around four hours each way.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I see flamingos at Lake Nakuru on a day trip?',
    a: 'Sometimes. Flamingo numbers at Lake Nakuru rise and fall with water levels and algae, and birds move between Rift Valley lakes such as Bogoria and Elementaita, so no operator can promise a pink shoreline on a given date. Lake Nakuru National Park is a reliable rhino sanctuary year round, which is the stronger reason to go.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Hell&rsquo;s Gate suitable for children?',
    a: 'Yes for most families. Hell&rsquo;s Gate National Park allows cycling and walking among zebra, giraffe and buffalo, and the gorge walk involves scrambling that older children usually enjoy. Tell us their ages and we will say honestly whether the gorge section suits your group.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long is the drive to Amboseli from Nairobi?',
    a: 'Amboseli National Park lies roughly 240 km southeast of Nairobi, and the drive takes around four hours each way depending on the route and road conditions. A single-day visit means eight hours of driving for a few hours in the park, so we will tell you plainly whether an overnight suits you better.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the best day trip for someone who has never been on safari?',
    a: 'Lake Nakuru National Park for wildlife density, or Lake Naivasha for variety in a shorter day. A first-timer with only one day and an early start gets more out of Nairobi National Park, because the drive is minutes rather than hours and the game viewing time is longer.',
  },
